\title{Small gaps in the surface of associated types}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-15}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{language}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-15}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\p{Three small points, each accepted or rejected in a way that surprises:}
\ul{
\li{An associated const cannot have an associated type as its type. \code{\verb~|const K: Self::A;~} is rejected with a message that a constant type is required, even when every impl binds \code{\verb~|A~} to a scalar.}
\li{Inside an impl, \code{\verb~|Self::Item { v: 1 }~} is reported as an unknown constructor although the impl binds \code{\verb~|Item~} to a record. In generic code \code{\verb~|T::Item { v: 1 }~} gets the same report with a suggestion of an unrelated record.}
\li{\code{\verb~|type Self;~} is accepted as the name of an associated type, and \code{\verb~|T::Self~} then works.}
}
\p{Found by the fuzzing of associated types recorded in [ses-TKVW](ses-TKVW).}
